/*Q22. You are developing a Student Search System using an array of objects.
Class: Student
Create a separate class named Student to represent an individual student. It must contain the following instance variables:
• id (int) — stores the student's ID.
• name (String) — stores the student's name.
The class must include:
• A parameterized constructor Student(int id, String name) to initialize the student's details.
• A display() method to display the student's ID and name in the required format.
Class: StudentSearch
Implement the StudentSearch class to manage student records and search for students using an array of Student objects.
The class must perform the following operations:
1. Read the number of students, n.
2. Create an array of Student objects of size n.
3. Read each student's ID and name, create a Student object, and store it in the array.
4. Read a search option:
• 1 — Search by student ID.
• 2 — Search by student name.
5. Read the corresponding search key.
6. Search the student records and display the matching details:
• When searching by ID, display only the first matching student.
• When searching by name, display all students whose names match exactly.
7. If no matching student is found, display "Student not found".
Input Format:
The input consists of:
• The first line contains an integer n, representing the number of students.
• The next 7 lines contain the student details:
• Student ID (int)
• Student Name (String)
• The next line contains an integer choice:
• 1 for searching by ID
• 2 for searching by Name
• The final line contains the search key:
• An integer student ID if the option is 1.
• A student name if the option is 2.
Output Format:
• Ifa matching student is found, display:
Student ID: <id>
Student Name: <name>
• If no matching student is found, display:
Student not found*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
class Student {
    int id;
    String name;
 
    Student(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }
 
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Student ID: " + id);
        System.out.println("Student Name: " + name);
    }
}
 
public class StudentSearch {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = Integer.parseInt(sc.nextLine().trim());
        Student[] students = new Student[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int id = Integer.parseInt(sc.nextLine().trim());
            String name = sc.nextLine().trim();
            students[i] = new Student(id, name);
        }
        int option = Integer.parseInt(sc.nextLine().trim());
        String key = sc.nextLine().trim();
        boolean found = false;
        if (option == 1) {
            int id = Integer.parseInt(key);
            for (Student s : students) {
                if (s.id == id) {
                    s.display();
                    found = true;
                    break;                     // only the first match
                }
            }
        } else if (option == 2) {
            for (Student s : students) {
                if (s.name.equals(key)) {      // all exact matches
                    s.display();
                    found = true;
                }
            }
        }
        if (!found) System.out.println("Student not found");
        sc.close();
    }
}
